    500MB cap — writes suspended at cap, resume after rotation

Pending Bugs Reported by previous claude sessions (needs to be rechecked)

Bug 1 — disconnect_ip() : _popped is None branch is logically inverted (code bug)
    File: Python · function disconnect_ip()
    What happens: When _popped is None, it means pipe()'s finally block already popped and cleaned up the entry before disconnect_ip() reached the pop. 
    The code should silently continue — instead it logs a fake "Forced disconnect" and increments killed, making the caller believe it disconnected something it did not. The history entry is correctly skipped (pipe already wrote it), so only the log line and the count are wrong.
    Correct behaviour: just continue.

Bug 2 — Main accept loop: AbuseIPDB auto-block entry is missing "type" and "expires" fields (code bug)
    File: Python · if __name__ == "__main__": accept loop
    Every other code path that appends to AUTO_BLOCKED_IPS includes "type" and "expires":
    In JS renderBlocked(), b.type being undefined falls silently to the auto tag — so the badge renders correctly. 
    But b.expires being undefined means the expires cell is rendered as — only because the JS isTemp branch is not taken. 
    If you ever add logic that reads b.expires for non-temp entries this will break.

Bug 3 — Context menu ctxDisc() bypasses the confirmation modal (UX/design bug)
    File: HTML/JS · function ctxDisc()
    discId() disconnects immediately with no confirmation. 
    The function confirmDiscId() (with a modal and Cancel button) already exists but is never called from the context menu. 
    The right-click menu is the primary way to disconnect in the current UI — every other destructive action (Disconnect All, Blacklist, Unblock) has a confirmation modal. 
    This one silently fires.

Bug 4 — abuse_lookup_cached() called inside the rulefile-block rejection path (design/performance bug)
    File: Python · if __name__ == "__main__": accept loop
    This was added in v28 to populate the score/country for the Session Blacklist table. 
    The problem: for every connection attempt from a rulefile-blocked IP, a (cached) AbuseIPDB lookup is triggered. 
    The 1-hour cache helps, but the very first hit per IP within any hour window makes a live network request (requests.get(...)) while the main accept loop is blocked on it — delaying the rejection and potentially all other new connections in flight. The score/country is cosmetic data for a UI table; 
    it should not be fetched in the hot path.

